MatchBox runs BoxLang on a Rust VM. This tour introduces the core language; use the Run buttons to try examples in your browser.

For runtime and target differences, see [BoxLang compatibility](/differences-from-boxlang/index.html). For the language in depth, use the [language reference](/reference/language/index.html). To learn by doing, start with the [course](/course/index.html).

## Variables, strings and operators

```boxlang
name = "MatchBox";
active = true;
count = 3;
println( "Hello, #name#!" );
println( "Built with " & name );
println( count * 2 + 1 );
println( "abc" contains "b" );
println( n ?: "n is null" );
```

Output:

```text
Hello, MatchBox!
Built with MatchBox
7
true
n is null
```

Variables are dynamically typed. `&` joins strings, `#expression#` interpolates inside a double-quoted string, and `?:` supplies a value when the left side is null. See [operators](/reference/language/operators/index.html).

## Arrays and structs

```boxlang
targets = [ "native", "browser", "embedded" ];
println( targets[ 1 ] );
targets.append( "wasi" );
println( targets.len() );

project = { name: "Hello", ready: true };
println( project.NAME );

lengths = arrayMap( targets, ( target ) => len( target ) );
println( serializeJSON( lengths ) );

{ name, ready } = project;
println( name & " " & ready );
println( serializeJSON( [ 0, ...targets ] ) );
```

Output:

```text
native
4
Hello
[6,7,8,4]
Hello true
[0,"native","browser","embedded","wasi"]
```

Arrays are 1-indexed and struct keys are case-insensitive. Destructuring and spread are covered in [destructuring, spread and ranges](/reference/language/destructuring-spread-ranges/index.html). MatchBox also has a [set type](/reference/language/sets/index.html).

## Control flow and functions

```boxlang
for ( target in [ "native", "browser" ] ) {
    if ( target == "native" ) {
        println( "Build an executable" );
    } else {
        println( "Explore " & target );
    }
}

switch ( "b" ) {
    case "a":
        println( "A" );
        break;
    case "b":
    case "c":
        println( "B or C" );
        break;
    default:
        println( "other" );
}

function greet( name = "world" ) {
    return "Hello, " & name;
}
double = ( number ) => number * 2;
println( greet() );
println( greet( name = "Ada" ) );
println( double( 21 ) );
```

Output:

```text
Build an executable
Explore browser
B or C
Hello, world
Hello, Ada
42
```

Use `while`, `do ... while`, `break`, `continue` and ranges for other loop shapes. Closures can use values from their enclosing function; see [scopes](/reference/language/scopes/index.html).

## Classes

```boxlang
class Product accessors="true" {
    property name;
    property price;

    function init() {
        return this;
    }
}

product = new Product();
product.setName( "Widget" );
println( product.getName() );
```

Output:

```text
Widget
```

Use `init` to initialize objects and `extends="Parent"` to inherit behavior. See [classes](/reference/language/classes/index.html).

## Errors and asynchronous work

```boxlang
try {
    throw( type = "MyError", message = "boom" );
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( e.type & ": " & e.message );
} finally {
    println( "done" );
}

future = runAsync( () => {
    sleep( 100 );
    return "finished";
} );
println( future.get() );
```

Output:

```text
MyError: boom
done
finished
```

A typed catch handles matching errors; `catch ( any e )` is a fallback. See [error handling](/reference/language/error-handling/index.html) and [async and futures](/reference/language/async-and-futures/index.html).

## Function library

MatchBox ships built-in functions for strings, arrays, structs, dates, JSON, regular expressions, XML, hashing and more. The [function reference](/reference/functions/index.html) lists each one. Some groups are optional parts of the build:

|          Group          |           Needs the build feature            |              In the browser              |
|-------------------------|----------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------|
| Files and directories   | `bif-io` (default)                           | No                                       |
| `http()`                | `bif-http` (default)                         | Not tested, see [HTTP](/http/index.html) |
| Hashing and encryption  | `bif-crypto` (default)                       | No (`hash` is not defined)               |
| Terminal helpers        | `bif-cli` (default)                          | No (`cliClear` is not defined)           |
| Java interop            | `bif-jni` (default)                          | No                                       |
| Zip and tar             | `bif-zip`                                    | Not tested                               |
| Queries and datasources | `bif-datasource`; `qoq` for query of queries | No                                       |

See [Building MatchBox](/getting-started/building-matchbox/index.html) for custom build options.

## Other parts of the language

* [Templates (.bxm)](/reference/language/templates-bxm/index.html): mix markup with BoxLang.
* [Modules](/reference/language/modules/index.html): add functions and classes with `--module`.
* [Queries and data](/reference/language/query-and-data/index.html).
* [Java interop](/reference/language/java-interop/index.html): experimental.
* [Native Fusion](/examples/native_fusion/README/index.html): call Rust from BoxLang.

An **array** is an ordered list. A **struct** is a set of named values, like an object or a dictionary. Between them they cover most of the data you will handle.

## Arrays

Array positions start at **1**, not 0. This surprises people coming from JavaScript or Rust, so it is worth a run.

```boxlang
colors = [ "red", "green", "blue" ];

println( colors[ 1 ] );
println( colors.len() );

colors.append( "violet" );
println( colors.toList( ", " ) );

colors[ 2 ] = "lime";
println( colors.toList( ", " ) );
println( colors[ 1 ] & " to " & colors[ colors.len() ] );
```

Output:

```text
red
3
red, green, blue, violet
red, lime, blue, violet
red to violet
```

`toList` turns an array into a delimited string, which is handy for printing. `append`, `prepend`, `pop` and `shift` add and remove items.

Sorting changes the array in place:

```boxlang
scores = [ 30, 4, 100, 12 ];
arraySort( scores, "numeric" );
println( scores.toList( "," ) );
```

Output:

```text
4,12,30,100
```

## Structs

```boxlang
person = { name: "Ada", city: "London" };

println( person.name );
println( person[ "city" ] );

person.age = 36;
println( structCount( person ) );
println( person.keyExists( "age" ) );
println( person.keyExists( "email" ) );

structDelete( person, "city" );
println( structCount( person ) );
```

Output:

```text
Ada
London
3
true
false
2
```

Reach a value with a dot or with brackets. Brackets let you use a key that is stored in a variable. Assigning to a new key adds it.

## Nesting

Arrays and structs nest freely, which is how most real data looks.

```boxlang
person = { name: "Ada", langs: [ "BoxLang", "Rust" ] };
println( person.langs[ 2 ] );

people = [
    { name: "Ada", age: 36 },
    { name: "Linus", age: 28 }
];

for ( p in people ) {
    println( p.name & " is " & p.age );
}
```

Output:

```text
Rust
Ada is 36
Linus is 28
```

## Loops

```boxlang
for ( n in [ 10, 20, 30 ] ) {
    println( n );
}

for ( i = 1; i <= 3; i++ ) {
    println( "round " & i );
}

total = 0;
for ( n in 1..4 ) {
    total += n;
}
println( total );

count = 0;
while ( count < 3 ) {
    count += 1;
}
println( count );

for ( key, value in { a: 1, b: 2 } ) {
    println( key & "=" & value );
}
```

Output:

```text
10
20
30
round 1
round 2
round 3
10
3
a=1
b=2
```

`1..4` is a range. `for ( key, value in struct )` walks a struct. Do not rely on the order of struct keys.

## Try it

Add a `println` inside the `people` loop that only prints people older than 30. You will need an `if`, which lesson 7 covers in depth, but you can guess the shape.

A future represents a result that may not be ready yet. `runAsync()` starts work and returns a future; `get()` waits for its result.

## Starting work

```boxlang
slow = runAsync( () => {
    sleep( 30 );
    return "slow";
} );
fast = runAsync( () => "fast" );

println( fast.get() & " then " & slow.get() );
println( runAsync( () => 4 ).then( value => value * 2 ).get() );
```

```text
fast then slow
8
```

Use `then()` to transform a result when it arrives. `asyncAllApply( items, callback )` applies a function to a collection and returns the results.

## Handling errors

Errors from asynchronous work surface when you collect the result. Use `try`/`catch`, or provide a recovery callback:

```boxlang
result = runAsync( () => {
    throw( message = "unavailable" );
} ).exceptionally( error => "fallback" );

println( result.get() );
```

```text
fallback
```

See [error handling](/reference/language/error-handling/index.html) for normal catch and cleanup patterns.

## Concurrency

BoxLang futures take turns on the VM's thread. Native I/O can run independently, but CPU-heavy BoxLang callbacks do not spread across multiple cores. `sleep()` yields to other tasks rather than making computation parallel.

The browser playground uses the same cooperative model and stops runs after five seconds. For a guided introduction, see the [async lesson](/course/08-async/index.html).

A class groups data with the methods that work on it. Define one inside a script or in a `.bx` file, then create an instance with `new`.

## Constructor, this and variables

`init()` initializes the object. Use `this` for public fields and `variables` for private instance state. Returning `this` also makes methods chainable.

```boxlang
class Counter {
    function init( start = 0 ) {
        variables.count = start;
        return this;
    }

    function increment() {
        variables.count += 1;
        return this;
    }

    function get() {
        return variables.count;
    }
}

println( new Counter( 5 ).increment().increment().get() );
```

```text
7
```

## File-based classes

Put a class in a file such as `models/Pet.bx`:

```boxlang
class {
    function init( name ) {
        this.name = name;
        return this;
    }

    function speak() {
        return "I am " & this.name;
    }
}
```

Import and use it from your application:

```boxlang
import models.Pet;
println( new Pet( "Rex" ).speak() );
```

File-based classes need a project filesystem; use inline classes in the browser playground.

## Inheritance and interfaces

Use `extends="Parent"` to inherit behavior and `implements="Interface"` to declare an interface. A child can override methods and call the parent implementation with `super.method()`.

```boxlang
class Animal {
    function speak() {
        return "hello";
    }
}

class Dog extends="Animal" {
    function speak() {
        return super.speak() & " from Dog";
    }
}

println( new Dog().speak() );
```

```text
hello from Dog
```

## Properties and accessors

Properties provide getter and setter methods:

```boxlang
class Person accessors="true" {
    property name;
}

person = new Person();
person.setName( "Ann" );
println( person.getName() );
```

```text
Ann
```

For a guided introduction, see the [classes lesson](/course/06-classes/index.html).

A **class** bundles data with the functions that work on it. You create an object from a class with `new`.

## A first class

```boxlang
class Counter {
    function init( start = 0 ) {
        variables.count = start;
        return this;
    }

    function increment() {
        variables.count = variables.count + 1;
        return this;
    }

    function get() {
        return variables.count;
    }
}

c = new Counter( 5 );
c.increment();
c.increment();
println( c.get() );
```

Output:

```text
7
```

* `init` is the **constructor** . It runs when you call `new Counter( 5 )`.
* `variables.count` is **private** state, stored inside the object. Code outside the class reaches it only through the methods.
* `this` is the object itself.

## Chaining

Because `increment` returns `this`, calls can be chained:

```boxlang
class Counter {
    function init( start = 0 ) {
        variables.count = start;
        return this;
    }

    function increment() {
        variables.count = variables.count + 1;
        return this;
    }

    function get() {
        return variables.count;
    }
}

println( new Counter().increment().increment().increment().get() );
```

Output:

```text
3
```

## Public data

Values you store on `this` are public. That is simple, but it means anyone can change them.

```boxlang
class Account {
    function init( owner, balance = 0 ) {
        this.owner = owner;
        variables.balance = balance;
        return this;
    }

    function deposit( amount ) {
        variables.balance = variables.balance + amount;
        return this;
    }

    function withdraw( amount ) {
        if ( amount > variables.balance ) {
            throw( type = "Funds", message = "Insufficient funds" );
        }
        variables.balance = variables.balance - amount;
        return this;
    }

    function getBalance() {
        return variables.balance;
    }
}

account = new Account( "Ada", 100 );
account.deposit( 50 ).withdraw( 30 );
println( account.owner & " has " & account.getBalance() );

try {
    account.withdraw( 500 );
} catch ( Funds e ) {
    println( "Refused: " & e.message );
}
```

Output:

```text
Ada has 120
Refused: Insufficient funds
```

Here the owner is public on `this`, while the balance is private, so the only way to change it is through `deposit` and `withdraw`, which enforce the rules. The `try` and `catch` at the bottom are the subject of the next lesson.

## A class that holds a collection

```boxlang
class Stack {
    function init() {
        variables.items = [];
        return this;
    }

    function push( value ) {
        variables.items.append( value );
        return this;
    }

    function pop() {
        return variables.items.pop();
    }

    function size() {
        return variables.items.len();
    }
}

s = new Stack().push( 1 ).push( 2 ).push( 3 );
println( s.pop() );
println( s.size() );
```

Output:

```text
3
2
```

## Going further

Explore inheritance, interfaces and properties in the [class reference](/reference/language/classes/index.html).

## Try it

Add a `reset()` method to `Counter` that sets the count back to zero and returns `this`.

Destructuring extracts values, spread expands collections, and ranges describe sequences for iteration.

## Destructuring

Pull values out of a struct or an array in one statement.

```boxlang
{ name, age } = { name: "Ann", age: 30 };
println( name & " is " & age );

{ name: who } = { name: "Bob" };
println( who );

[ first, second ] = [ 10, 20, 30 ];
println( first & "," & second );

{ a, b } = { a: 1 };
println( isNull( b ) );
```

Output:

```text
Ann is 30
Bob
10,20
true
```

* A struct pattern matches by key. `{ name: who }` renames.
* A key that is missing from the struct leaves the variable null.
* Destructuring works inside functions too.

Use `...rest` to collect remaining values:

```boxlang
[ first, ...rest ] = [ 10, 20, 30 ];
println( first );
println( serializeJSON( rest ) );
```

```text
10
[20,30]
```

## Spread

`...` expands an array into an array literal or a call, and a struct into a struct literal.

```boxlang
base = [ 1, 2 ];
println( serializeJSON( [ 0, ...base, 3 ] ) );
println( serializeJSON( [ ...base, ...base ] ) );

defaults = { color: "red" };
println( serializeJSON( { ...defaults, size: 9 } ) );

function add( p, q ) {
    return p + q;
}
println( add( ...[ 3, 4 ] ) );
```

Output:

```text
[0,1,2,3]
[1,2,1,2]
{"color":"red","size":9}
7
```

To override existing struct keys, use `structAppend` rather than spreading a duplicate key into a literal:

```boxlang
merged = duplicate( defaults );
structAppend( merged, { color: "blue" } );
```

## Ranges

`a..b` is an inclusive range and `a..<b` stops before `b`. Both are meant for `for ... in`.

```boxlang
for ( i in 1..3 ) {
    println( "up " & i );
}
for ( i in 3..1 ) {
    println( "down " & i );
}
for ( i in 1..<4 ) {
    println( "short " & i );
}
n = 3;
for ( i in 1..n ) {
    println( "var " & i );
}
total = 0;
for ( i in 1..100 ) {
    total += i;
}
println( total );
```

Output:

```text
up 1
up 2
up 3
down 3
down 2
down 1
short 1
short 2
short 3
var 1
var 2
var 3
5050
```

Ranges are useful in loops; use `arraySlice( array, start, length )` for a portion of an array. If an exclusive range might be empty, check the bounds before iterating.

See also [operators](/reference/language/operators/index.html) and [sets](/reference/language/sets/index.html).

Use `try` and `catch` to handle errors, and `finally` for cleanup. The [errors lesson](/course/07-errors-and-control-flow/index.html) introduces the basics.

## try, catch, finally

```boxlang
try {
    throw( type = "MyError", message = "boom", detail = "more", errorCode = "E1" );
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( e.type & " | " & e.message & " | " & e.detail & " | " & e.errorCode );
} finally {
    println( "finally runs" );
}

try {
    x = 1 / 0;
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( e.type & ": " & e.message );
}

try {
    throw "plain text";
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( e.message );
}

try {
    try {
        throw "inner";
    } catch ( any e ) {
        rethrow;
    }
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( "outer got " & e.message );
}

function f() {
    try {
        return "from try";
    } finally {
        println( "cleanup" );
    }
}
println( f() );
```

Output:

```text
MyError | boom | more | E1
finally runs
ExpressionException: Division by zero
plain text
outer got inner
cleanup
from try
```

* The caught value is a struct. It holds only what `throw` was given, plus `stackTrace`. `throw( type = "A" )` gives `e.message` as null, and `structKeyExists( e, "type" )` is `false` for `throw( message = "x" )`.
* Errors raised by the VM have `e.type` set to `ExpressionException`, for example division by zero or calling an undefined function.
* `catch ( e )` without the `any` keyword is accepted.
* An uncaught error prints `Error: ...` with the source line and the CLI exits with status 1.

## Typed catches

The first matching catch runs. Types are case-insensitive: `Custom` also matches `Custom.Input`, but not `CustomInput`. Use `any` as a fallback. Errors with no matching catch propagate outward, after `finally` runs.

```boxlang
try {
    throw( type = "Custom.Input", message = "bad value" );
} catch ( Other e ) {
    println( "wrong handler" );
} catch ( "custom" e ) {
    println( e.message );
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( "fallback" );
}
```

```text
bad value
```

You can also inspect `e.type` inside a catch when handling several cases together.

```boxlang
function risky( kind ) {
    throw( type = kind, message = "failed: " & kind );
}

for ( kind in [ "NotFound", "Denied", "Other" ] ) {
    try {
        risky( kind );
    } catch ( any e ) {
        if ( e.type == "NotFound" ) {
            println( "404 " & e.message );
        } else if ( e.type == "Denied" ) {
            println( "403 " & e.message );
        } else {
            println( "unhandled " & e.type );
        }
    }
}
```

Output:

```text
404 failed: NotFound
403 failed: Denied
unhandled Other
```

## Asynchronous errors

An error inside a `runAsync` callback surfaces when you call `get()`. See [async and futures](/reference/language/async-and-futures/index.html).

## Choosing a path

```boxlang
temperature = 22;

if ( temperature > 30 ) {
    println( "hot" );
} else if ( temperature > 15 ) {
    println( "pleasant" );
} else {
    println( "cold" );
}

day = 3;
switch ( day ) {
    case 1:
        println( "Monday" );
        break;
    case 3:
        println( "Wednesday" );
        break;
    default:
        println( "some other day" );
}
```

Output:

```text
pleasant
Wednesday
```

`switch` needs a `break` at the end of each case, otherwise execution falls through into the next one.

## Skipping and stopping

`continue` skips to the next round of a loop, and `break` leaves the loop.

```boxlang
for ( i = 1; i <= 6; i++ ) {
    if ( i == 2 ) {
        continue;
    }
    if ( i == 5 ) {
        break;
    }
    println( i );
}
```

Output:

```text
1
3
4
```

## Handling errors

When something goes wrong, code **throws** an error. If nothing catches it, the script stops. A `try` block lets you catch the error and carry on.

```boxlang
try {
    println( "before" );
    throw "something broke";
    println( "never printed" );
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( "caught: " & e.message );
} finally {
    println( "always runs" );
}
```

Output:

```text
before
caught: something broke
always runs
```

`catch ( any e )` catches every kind of error. `finally` runs whether or not an error happened, which makes it the right place for cleanup.

## Built-in errors

The runtime throws errors of its own, for example when you divide by zero.

```boxlang
try {
    println( 10 / 0 );
} catch ( any e ) {
    println( "caught: " & e.message );
}
```

Output:

```text
caught: Division by zero
```

## Your own error types

Give an error a **type** so callers can catch exactly the problems they know how to handle.

```boxlang
function parseAge( text ) {
    if ( !isNumeric( text ) ) {
        throw( type = "BadInput", message = "'" & text & "' is not a number" );
    }
    return text + 0;
}

try {
    println( parseAge( "36" ) );
    println( parseAge( "old" ) );
} catch ( BadInput e ) {
    println( e.type & ": " & e.message );
}
```

Output:

```text
36
BadInput: 'old' is not a number
```

## Try it

Change the second call to `parseAge( "42" )` and run again. Then add a `catch ( any e )` after the `BadInput` one and throw a different kind of error to see which handler runs.

## Functions

```boxlang
function greet( name, greeting = "Hello" ) {
    return greeting & ", " & name;
}

println( greet( "Ada" ) );
println( greet( "Ada", "Hi" ) );
println( greet( greeting = "Hey", name = "Linus" ) );
```

Output:

```text
Hello, Ada
Hi, Ada
Hey, Linus
```

A parameter with `=` has a default. You can also pass arguments **by name**, in any order.

Functions can call themselves:

```boxlang
function factorial( n ) {
    return n <= 1 ? 1 : n * factorial( n - 1 );
}

println( factorial( 10 ) );
```

Output:

```text
3628800
```

## Arrow functions

An arrow function is a short, nameless function. It is often passed to another function.

```boxlang
double = n => n * 2;
add = ( a, b ) => a + b;

println( double( 4 ) );
println( add( 2, 3 ) );
```

Output:

```text
8
5
```

## Map, filter and reduce

Arrays come with three workhorses. `map` transforms every item, `filter` keeps some of them, and `reduce` folds the array down to one value.

```boxlang
words = [ "box", "lang", "match" ];

println( words.map( w => w.ucase() ).toList( " " ) );
println( words.filter( w => w.len() > 3 ).toList( "," ) );
println( words.reduce( ( total, w ) => total + w.len(), 0 ) );

nums = [ 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6 ];
sumOfSquares = nums
    .filter( n => n % 2 == 0 )
    .map( n => n * n )
    .reduce( ( a, n ) => a + n, 0 );
println( sumOfSquares );
```

Output:

```text
BOX LANG MATCH
lang,match
12
56
```

`each`, `every` and `some` work the same way. `every` is true when the callback is true for all items, `some` when it is true for at least one.

```boxlang
println( [ 1, 2, 3 ].every( n => n > 0 ) );
println( [ 1, 2, 3 ].some( n => n > 2 ) );
[ "a", "b" ].each( v => println( "item " & v ) );
```

Output:

```text
true
true
item a
item b
```

## Functions as values

A function is a value, so you can pass one to another function.

```boxlang
function apply( fn, value ) {
    return fn( value );
}

println( apply( s => s.ucase(), "shout" ) );
println( apply( n => n + 1, 41 ) );
```

Output:

```text
SHOUT
42
```

