## Limitations

* All targets support Fusion, but Rust APIs and dependencies must match the target.
* Use `--target js` for browsers and `--target wasi` for WASI hosts.
* Return types other than `f64` from `#[matchbox_fn]` need a manual BIF.
* Native object property access requires `get_property` in `BxNativeObject`.

See [native-builds.md](/building-and-deploying/native-builds/index.html) for the complete native build reference
and [differences-from-boxlang.md](/differences-from-boxlang/index.html) for the full
list of features not available in MatchBox.

BoxLang provides symbolic and word operators for calculations, comparisons and control flow.

## Arithmetic and strings

|           Operator            |                 Meaning                  |     Example      | Result |
|-------------------------------|------------------------------------------|------------------|--------|
| `+` `-` `*` `/`               | Arithmetic                               | `10 / 4`         | `2.5`  |
| `%`, `mod`                    | Remainder, sign follows the left operand | `-7 % 3`         | `-1`   |
| `\`                           | Integer division                         | `10 \ 3`         | `3`    |
| `^`                           | Power                                    | `2 ^ 10`         | `1024` |
| `&`                           | String concatenation                     | `"abc" & 1`      | `abc1` |
| `+=` `-=` `*=` `/=` `%=` `&=` | Compound assignment                      | `x = 10; x %= 3` | `1`    |
| `++` `--`                     | Increment and decrement                  | `i++`            |        |

Use `+` for arithmetic and `&` for joining text. Numeric strings can participate in arithmetic: `"5" + "6"` is `11`. Division by zero throws an error.

## Comparison and logic

```boxlang
println( 5 == 5 );
println( 5 eq 5 );
println( 5 neq 4 );
println( 5 != 4 );
println( 3 lte 3 );
println( 3 gte 4 );
println( "a" < "b" );
println( 1 is 1 );
println( "abc" contains "B" );
println( "abc" does not contain "x" );
println( true && false || true );
println( true and false );
println( true xor false );
println( 1 eqv 1 );
println( !false );
```

Output:

```text
true
true
true
true
true
false
true
true
true
true
true
false
true
true
true
```

String comparison ignores case: `"a" == "A"` is `true`. `===` behaves like `==`, which BoxLang does too.

Use `!` or `not` for negation, `&&` or `and` for conjunction, and `||` or `or` for alternatives.

## Bitwise

BoxLang spells bitwise operators with a `b` prefix.

```boxlang
println( 5 b& 3 );
println( 5 b| 3 );
println( 5 b^ 3 );
println( b~ 5 );
println( 1 b<< 4 );
println( 256 b>> 4 );
println( 16 b>>> 2 );
```

Output:

```text
1
7
6
-6
16
16
4
```

Both operands must be numbers.

## castAs and instanceOf

`castAs` takes the type name as a string. Wrap it in parentheses when you combine it with other operators, because the string after `castAs` swallows what follows.

```boxlang
println( ( 5 castAs "string" ) & "!" );
println( ( "3.5" castAs "numeric" ) + 1 );
println( "yes" castAs "boolean" );
println( "a" instanceOf "string" );
println( 1 instanceOf "numeric" );
println( { } instanceOf "struct" );
println( [ ] instanceOf "array" );
println( ( x => x ) instanceOf "function" );
println( 1 instanceOf "string" );
```

Output:

```text
5!
4.5
true
true
true
true
true
true
false
```

* `"abc" castAs "numeric"` throws `BoxCastException: Could not cast object [abc] to type [numeric]`.
* `7.9 castAs "integer"` throws a `BoxCastException`. BoxLang does too.
* `x castAs string` without quotes fails.
* `setValue instanceOf "set"` is `false` for a [set](/reference/language/sets/index.html).

## Elvis, ternary and safe navigation

```boxlang
n = null;
println( n ?: "was null" );
println( "" ?: "not used" );
println( 5 > 3 ? "yes" : "no" );

s = { a: { b: 2 } };
println( s?.a?.b );
println( s.missing?.deeper ?: "safe" );
println( isNull( n?.a ) );
```

Output:

```text
was null

yes
2
safe
true
```

`?:` only replaces null. An empty string passes through, so the second line prints an empty line.

A `?.` guards only the step it is on. `n?.a.b` with `n` null throws `Member access only supported on structs, instances, JS objects, and native objects`. BoxLang returns null. Write `n?.a?.b`.

For sets and ranges see [sets](/reference/language/sets/index.html) and [destructuring, spread and ranges](/reference/language/destructuring-spread-ranges/index.html).

A query is a table of rows and named columns. You can build one in memory or obtain one from a database.

Database functions are available in native builds with datasource support, not in the browser playground.

## In-memory queries

```boxlang
people = queryNew(
    "id,name",
    "integer,varchar",
    [ [ 1, "Ann" ], [ 2, "Bob" ] ]
);

println( people.recordCount );
println( people.name[ 2 ] );

for ( person in people ) {
    println( person.id & ":" & person.name );
}
```

```text
2
Bob
1:Ann
2:Bob
```

Functions such as `queryAddRow`, `queryGetCell`, `querySetCell` and `queryColumnData` let you work with rows and columns directly. Query indexes start at 1.

## Datasources

Register a datasource, then pass its name to `queryExecute`. MatchBox provides SQLite and PostgreSQL drivers.

```boxlang
datasourceRegister( "main", {
    driver: "sqlite",
    database: "app.db"
} );

people = queryExecute(
    "select id, name from people where id = ?",
    [ 1 ],
    { datasource: "main" }
);
```

The example assumes the database has a `people` table. Use query parameters rather than joining user input into SQL. Datasources can also be declared in [`matchbox.toml`](/reference/configuration/index.html#matchboxtoml).

## Transactions and query of queries

Use `transactionBegin`, `transactionCommit` and `transactionRollback` to group database changes.

Query of queries runs SQL over in-memory query data using `dbtype: "query"`; it requires a build with the `qoq` feature. See [source builds](/getting-started/building-matchbox/index.html#cargo-features) for custom runtime features and the [query function reference](/reference/functions/queries-and-datasources/index.html) for available functions.

A **future** stands for a value that is not ready yet. `runAsync` starts some work and hands you a future straight away. You call `get()` on it when you need the result.

## Your first future

```boxlang
answer = runAsync( () => 6 * 7 );

println( "started" );
println( answer.get() );
```

Output:

```text
started
42
```

## Several at once

Start more than one future, then collect the results.

```boxlang
slow = runAsync( () => {
    sleep( 20 );
    return "slow";
} );
fast = runAsync( () => "fast" );

println( fast.get() & " then " & slow.get() );
```

Output:

```text
fast then slow
```

## Chaining

`then` runs a function on the result when it arrives, and gives you a new future.

```boxlang
result = runAsync( () => 10 ).then( n => n * 2 );
println( result.get() );
```

Output:

```text
20
```

## Measuring time

`getTickCount()` returns milliseconds, and `sleep( ms )` pauses.

```boxlang
start = getTickCount();
sleep( 30 );
elapsed = getTickCount() - start;
println( elapsed >= 30 );
```

Output:

```text
true
```

## In the browser

BoxLang futures take turns on the VM's thread, in both native and browser builds. Native I/O can run independently; CPU-heavy callbacks do not run across multiple cores. The playground stops runs after five seconds.

## Try it

Start three futures that each `sleep` for a different time and return a different word. Print them in the order you started them.

A set holds each value once. Create one with a literal, `setNew()`, `setOf()` or `toSet()`.

## Creating a set

```boxlang
a = set{ 3, 1, 2, 3 };
println( a.size() );

empty = set{};
println( empty.isEmpty() );

b = setOf( "x", "y", "x" );
println( b.toList( "," ) );

c = [ 5, 5, 6 ].toSet();
println( c.toList( "," ) );

d = setNew( type = "linked", values = [ 3, 1, 2, 3 ] );
println( d.toList( "," ) );
```

Output:

```text
3
true
x,y
5,6
3,1,2
```

`set{}` is a literal: the word `set` followed by braces. `setNew()` takes `type` (`default`, `linked` or `sorted`) and `values`. `listToSet( "a,b,a" )` and `structKeySet( struct )` also return sets.

## Order

|         Type         |                                                 Ordering                                                 |
|----------------------|----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| default and `linked` | Insertion order.                                                                                         |
| `sorted`             | Sorted once, when `setNew()` builds it from `values`. Values you `add()` later are appended, not sorted. |

```boxlang
s = setNew( type = "sorted", values = [ 10, 9, 100 ] );
println( s.toList( "," ) );
s.add( 1 );
println( s.toList( "," ) );
```

Output:

```text
9,10,100
9,10,100,1
```

A set is stored as an array that skips duplicates when it is built. `isArray( s )` is `true`, `s[ 1 ]` reads the first value, `println( s )` prints it like an array and `serializeJSON( s )` returns an array. `s instanceOf "set"` is `false`.

## Operators and algebra

When either operand is a set, `+`, `-`, `*` and `^` do set algebra, and the other operand can be an array.

| Operator |       Member function        |            Result             |
|----------|------------------------------|-------------------------------|
| `a + b`  | `a.union( b )`               | Values in either set.         |
| `a - b`  | `a.difference( b )`          | Values in `a` and not in `b`. |
| `a * b`  | `a.intersection( b )`        | Values in both.               |
| `a ^ b`  | `a.symmetricDifference( b )` | Values in exactly one.        |

```boxlang
a = setOf( 1, 2, 3 );
b = setOf( 2, 3, 4 );

println( ( a + b ).toList( "," ) );
println( ( a - b ).toList( "," ) );
println( ( a * b ).toList( "," ) );
println( ( a ^ b ).toList( "," ) );

println( a.isSubsetOf( setOf( 1, 2, 3, 4 ) ) );
println( a.isSupersetOf( setOf( 1 ) ) );
println( a.isDisjointFrom( setOf( 9 ) ) );
println( ( a + [ 3, 7 ] ).toList( "," ) );
```

Output:

```text
1,2,3,4
1
2,3
1,4
true
true
true
1,2,3,7
```

Only an array works as the loose right operand. `a + "7,8"` is string concatenation and gives `[1,2]7,8`, and `[ 9 ] + a` is an array join.

## Member functions

|     Group      |                                                                        Works                                                                        |
|----------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Add and remove | `add`, `append`, `addAll`, `remove`, `delete`, `removeAll`, `retainAll`, `clear`                                                                    |
| Query          | `contains`, `has`, `size`, `len`, `length`, `count`, `isEmpty`, `isSubsetOf`, `isSupersetOf`, `isDisjointFrom`, `isCaseSensitive`, `isSynchronized` |
| Algebra        | `union`, `intersection`, `difference`, `symmetricDifference`                                                                                        |
| Functional     | `each`, `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, `every`, `some`, `none`, `find`, `reject`                                                                        |
| Convert        | `toArray`, `toList( delimiter )`                                                                                                                    |

```boxlang
s = setOf( 1, 2, 3, 4 );
println( s.map( x => x * 10 ).toList( "," ) );
println( s.filter( x => x > 2 ).toList( "," ) );
println( s.reduce( ( total, x ) => total + x, 0 ) );
println( s.every( x => x > 0 ) );
println( s.some( x => x > 3 ) );
println( s.none( x => x > 9 ) );
println( s.find( x => x > 2 ) );
```

Output:

```text
10,20,30,40
3,4
10
true
true
true
3
```

## Adding unique values

Use `union` or `+` when adding values that may already exist. These operations preserve uniqueness; direct append-style methods can introduce duplicates.

See also [destructuring, spread and ranges](/reference/language/destructuring-spread-ranges/index.html) and [operators](/reference/language/operators/index.html).

## Variables

You create a variable by assigning to it. There is no declaration keyword, and a variable can hold any type.

```boxlang
name = "Ada";
age = 36;
isAdmin = true;

println( name );
println( age + 1 );
println( isAdmin );
```

Output:

```text
Ada
37
true
```

## Strings and interpolation

Put `#expression#` inside a double-quoted string to insert a value. The expression can be any code. Use `&` to join strings.

```boxlang
name = "Ada";
age = 36;

println( "#name# is #age# years old" );
println( "Next year: #age + 1#" );
println( "Hello, " & name & "!" );
```

Output:

```text
Ada is 36 years old
Next year: 37
Hello, Ada!
```

To print a literal `#`, double it: `"Issue ##42"` prints `Issue #42`.

## String functions

Most string functions work both ways: `ucase( text )` or `text.ucase()`.

```boxlang
title = "  MatchBox  ";

println( title.trim() );
println( title.trim().ucase() );
println( title.trim().lcase() );
println( title.trim().len() );
println( title.trim().left( 5 ) );
println( title.trim().reverse() );
println( "hello world".replace( "world", "MatchBox" ) );
println( ucFirst( "hello" ) );
```

Output:

```text
MatchBox
MATCHBOX
matchbox
8
Match
xoBhctaM
hello MatchBox
Hello
```

## Numbers and operators

```boxlang
println( 10 / 4 );
println( 10 \ 4 );
println( 10 % 4 );
println( 2 ^ 10 );
println( 3 > 2 );
println( true && !false );
println( 3 > 2 ? "yes" : "no" );
println( "5" + 5 );
```

Output:

```text
2.5
2
2
1024
true
true
yes
10
```

`/` is normal division, `\` divides and drops the remainder, `%` is the remainder and `^` is a power. Notice that `"5" + 5` is `10`: BoxLang converts a numeric string to a number for arithmetic, while `&` always joins text.

## Missing values

`null` means "no value". The elvis operator `?:` supplies a default when the left side is null.

```boxlang
nickname = null;

println( nickname ?: "no nickname" );
println( isNull( nickname ) );
```

Output:

```text
no nickname
true
```

